In Spring, the process of rolling back a transaction when an exception occurs outside of the Spring repository involves using transaction management and the appropriate configuration. By default, Spring uses declarative transaction management, which allows you to define transaction boundaries declaratively using annotations or XML configuration.

Here's an approach you can follow to roll back a Spring repository save when an exception is thrown outside of the repository:

1. Enable transaction management: Ensure that transaction management is enabled in your Spring application. This can be done by configuring the appropriate transaction manager bean, such as `DataSourceTransactionManager` for database transactions.

2. Define transaction boundaries: Specify the boundaries of your transaction by annotating the service method that calls the repository save operation with the `@Transactional` annotation. This annotation can be applied to class-level or method-level, depending on your requirements.

   For example:
   ```java
   @Transactional
   public void saveEntity(Entity entity) {
       // Repository save operation
       repository.save(entity);
   }
   ```

3. Handle exceptions: Make sure to catch any exceptions that occur outside of the repository, either in your service layer or any higher layer. In the catch block, you can either log the exception or perform any necessary error handling.

4. Trigger rollback: To trigger a rollback when an exception occurs, you can re-throw the caught exception or throw a new exception from your catch block. By default, Spring's transaction management will roll back the transaction when a runtime exception is thrown.

   For example:
   ```java
   @Transactional
   public void saveEntity(Entity entity) {
       try {
           // Repository save operation
           repository.save(entity);
       } catch (Exception ex) {
           // Log the exception or perform error handling
           throw ex; // or throw a new exception
       }
   }
   ```

By following this approach, when an exception occurs outside of the Spring repository, the transaction will be marked for rollback, and any changes made during the repository save operation will be rolled back.

It's worth noting that for this approach to work, you need to ensure that your Spring repository is invoked within the transactional boundaries defined by the `@Transactional` annotation.
